\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Equilibria are roots of one current}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For the parameters of the \vocabk{Hodgkin–Huxley system}{fnip-0071},
let #{F(V)}, the steady current, be the ionic current of
\ref{fnip-0073}, with #{n}, #{m}, and #{h} at their steady values
for #{V}. A state #{(V,n,m,h)} is an \vocabk{equilibrium}{fnip-0014} if
and only if those three variables equal the steady values and
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=F(V)}.
\citet{(3.3), p. 64}{ermentrout2010mathematical} is this balance.}

\p{For the Morris–Lecar velocity of \ref{fnip-0005}, in any column
of \citet{Table 3.1, p. 51}{ermentrout2010mathematical}, a state
#{(V,n)} is an equilibrium if and only if #{n=n_{\infty}(V)} and
##{I_{\mathrm{app}}
   =g_{\mathrm{L}}(V-E_{\mathrm{L}})
   +g_{\mathrm{K}} n_{\infty}(V)(V-E_{\mathrm{K}})
   +g_{\mathrm{Ca}} m_{\infty}(V)(V-E_{\mathrm{Ca}}).}}

\p{A fold of either steady current is an applied current at which
the number of equilibria changes.}

\proof{
\p{In the Hodgkin–Huxley system the temperature factor is
#{1}, so each variable #{x} among #{n}, #{m}, and #{h} satisfies
the [activation equation](fnip-0071). In that definition,
#{\beta_{n}}, #{\beta_{m}}, and #{\alpha_{h}} are positive
multiples of exponentials, and #{\beta_{h}} is the reciprocal of
one plus an exponential, so #{\beta_{h}} is positive.
#{\alpha_{n}} and #{\alpha_{m}} equal the positive limits recorded
there at #{V=-55} and #{V=-40}. At every other voltage they are
positive multiples of #{u/(1-e^{-u})}, and that quotient is
positive for every real #{u} other than #{0}. Each sum of a rate
and its partner is therefore positive, and each time constant is
positive. The derivative #{\dot x} vanishes if and only if #{x}
equals its steady value. With those three variables at their
steady values, the voltage equation is
#{C\dot V=I_{\mathrm{app}}-F(V)}. Since #{C} is positive,
#{\dot V} vanishes if and only if #{I_{\mathrm{app}}=F(V)}.
The state is an equilibrium if and only if both conditions hold.}

\p{In the Morris–Lecar equation the factor #{\phi} is positive in
every column of that table, and \ref{fnip-0005} records that
#{\cosh((V-V_{3})/(2V_{4}))\ge 1}, so the time constant of #{n}
is positive. Thus #{\dot n} vanishes if and only if
#{n=n_{\infty}(V)}. With #{n} at that value, a positive
capacitance times #{\dot V} equals #{I_{\mathrm{app}}} minus the
three ionic currents in the display. Thus #{\dot V} vanishes if
and only if the displayed balance holds, and the state is an
equilibrium if and only if both vanishings hold.}
}
